-- title:
--   Norm of a local equation generates the image maximal ideal
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ and $B$ be commutative rings with $B$ an $A$-algebra that is free and finite as an $A$-module, let $K$ be a field, and let $\chi\colon B \to K$ be a ring homomorphism whose restriction $\chi \circ \mathrm{algebraMap}_{A,B}\colon A \to K$ is surjective. Suppose $g \in B$ generates the kernel of $\chi$, i.e. $\ker \chi = (g)$, and let $\mathfrak{m}$ be an ideal of $A$ equal to $\ker(\chi \circ \mathrm{algebraMap}_{A,B})$ and assumed maximal. Suppose further that there is a ring $A_{\mathfrak m}$, an $A$-algebra realising the localisation of $A$ at the prime $\mathfrak m$, which is a domain and a discrete valuation ring. Then the ideal of $A$ generated by $\mathrm{Nm}_{B/A}(g)$ — the determinant over $A$ of multiplication by $g$ on the finite free $A$-module $B$, i.e. Mathlib's `Algebra.norm A g` — is exactly $\mathfrak{m}$. Note that the hypotheses are imposed on $A$ only at the single maximal ideal $\mathfrak m$, no regularity being assumed elsewhere.
--
--   This is the affine-local computation behind the statement that the norm of the ideal sheaf of a $K$-rational point of a finite locally free cover is the ideal sheaf of its image: the norm of a local equation of the point upstairs is a local equation of the point downstairs. It is used in the construction of the isomorphism [`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.nonempty_normModule_invModule_ker_iso`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.nonempty_normModule_invModule_ker_iso).
-- source:
